# The toolchains this repository compares rucc against, pinned.
#
# One record per line, tab separated, in this order:
#
#   tool  version  host  sha256  url
#
# The host is the machine the toolchain runs on, spelled the way the tool spells it. Nothing here
# is fetched without a hash, and nothing here is fetched from a URL that is not on this line, so a
# new toolchain is a commit somebody reviewed rather than a download that happened.
#
# `toolchains/install <tool>` reads this. `toolchains/record-hash <url>` downloads once and prints
# the line to paste, which is how an entry gets added without anyone typing a hash from memory.
#
# The zig hashes are the ones published in https://ziglang.org/download/index.json for 0.16.0,
# released 13 April 2026. That release is the one spec/cross-compile/01-research-2026.md in the
# compiler repository was written against, so it is the version the parity claim is measured on
# until somebody moves it deliberately.
#
# Two toolchains are deliberately absent and the reason is the same for both. GCC 16 cross
# compilers and qemu are installed from the platform's package manager by `toolchains/install`,
# because upstream ships neither as a hash pinned binary for every host we run on, and building
# either from source in CI costs more than the reproducibility buys. What we do instead is record
# the version that was actually used in every facts file, so a result that changes because the
# toolchain changed is visible rather than mysterious. Pinned tarballs for both are wanted, and
# the way in is `toolchains/record-hash`.
#
# wasi-sdk is the reference for the wasm rows, because zig has one spelling for the three WASI
# previews and the compiler repository holds its wasm macros against clang 23 from wasi-sdk 34. The
# hashes are the digests that GitHub publishes for the wasi-sdk-34 release assets, and the arm64
# macOS one was checked against a download. wasi-sdk spells an aarch64 host arm64, and the host
# column here is still the zig spelling.

